message: "If you use this formalization, cite it via this metadata."
title: "hadamard-b-formal: a Lean formalization of the bordered Goethals-Seidel characterisation and the s = 1 border system"
abstract: >-
  A Lean 4 and Mathlib formalization of the theorem layer of the Hadamard-B
  note. It proves an exact if-and-only-if characterisation of when a
  Goethals-Seidel array over a finite abelian group extends to a Hadamard
  matrix through a border whose strips are constant on the cosets of a
  subgroup; the house form of that characterisation, whose hypothesis is
  exactly a two-tier periodic-autocorrelation profile, together with the
  classical Goethals-Seidel theorem over an arbitrary abelian group and the
  Wallis-Whiteman/Spence bordered construction, both at an arbitrary
  reflection shift; the two row-sum consequences D5 and D6; Lemma T, the
  s = 1 profile bijection, and the theorem that a twist fixing the reflection
  shift is a diagonal conjugation; the complete resolution of the s = 1,
  index-two border system (Theorem D), including the forced Gram and border
  tables, the collapse of the border condition to a single 4x4 equation, the
  integer Parseval identity, and transport across the doubling; and the
  index-two seed-problem collapse. Two witness records are imported under
  SHA-256 pins and checked in the kernel against the hypotheses of the
  characterisation, carrying Hadamard matrices of orders 52 and 20 through the
  theorem rather than through a matrix product; no novelty of existence is
  claimed at either order, both of which are long settled. All twenty-two
  public theorems are kernel-checked with axioms confined to propext,
  Classical.choice, and Quot.sound. AI-generated formalization with a human
  managing the workflow - produced by Claude Code (Fable 5, Anthropic) with
  Claude Opus subagent lanes; see DISCLOSURE.md. The authors field below
  records the repository owner and responsible publisher; the AI/human
  division of labour is DISCLOSURE.md. The single license below covers the
  code, which is the artifact; the prose documents are CC BY-SA 4.0, assigned
  by category in LICENSE-DOCS.md.
